- reference_cohort_and_imaging.nm_vldtn_diff_domain: {"answer": "Yes", "confidence_rating": "High", "negative_answer_category": "Not Negative", "reason": "The study validates the normative model by applying it to a different domain: pediatric epilepsy patients undergoing hemispherotomy (n=57; 29 left and 28 right hemispherotomy). These patients represent a clinically distinct population from the healthy children used for training. The validation is demonstrated through: (1) biologically plausible deviation patterns showing overall GM shrinkage/underdevelopment in patients compared to healthy norms (79% and 71% of left and right hemispherotomy patients had >80% of voxels with negative Z-scores preoperatively); (2) detection of longitudinal GMV changes that show both increases (contralateral cerebrum, ipsilateral cerebellum) and decreases (contralateral cerebellum) consistent with expected neuroplastic and neurodegenerative mechanisms post-surgery; (3) clinical validity demonstrated by significant correlation between GMV changes in contralateral cerebellum and behavioral improvement in right hemispherotomy patients (r=-0.64, p=0.007); and (4) predictive validity showing that preoperative GMV features significantly predict postoperative behavioral scores (left: r=0.52, p=0.006; right: r=0.48, p=0.021). These findings indicate that the normative model successfully generalizes to a clinically relevant different-domain population and captures meaningful individual-level deviations.", "supporting_text": "In most patients in the two groups, both preoperative and postoperative age-adjusted GMV values were negative across the vast majority of the unaffected regions, suggesting an overall shrinking pattern and underdevelopment of GM compared with the healthy children (Figure 2). Specifically, 79 and 59% of left hemispherotomy patients had more than 80% voxels showing a negative value before and after the surgery, respectively. In the right hemispherotomy group, 71 and 54% of patients had more than 80% of voxels showing a negative value before and after the surgery, respectively. (p.154-155) ... the change in age-adjusted GMV in the contralateral cerebellum was negatively correlated with the change in overall behavioral score in the right hemispherotomy patients (r=-0.64, p=0.007) but not in the left hemispherotomy patients (r=-0.10, p=0.73). (p.176-177) ... all three algorithms can significantly predict the postoperative behavioral score at the individual level (Table 2). (p.208-209)", "page_line": "p.154-177, p.208-209"}
- normative_modeling.nm_vldtn_handle_ns_detail: "Age and sex effects (nuisance structure) were explicitly modeled as covariates in the Gaussian process regression to derive age- and sex-adjusted GMV Z-scores. This normative modeling approach controls for developmental effects and sex differences. However, multi-site/scanner effects were not explicitly addressed despite using data from 11 datasets across multiple sites with different scanners (1.5T and 3T). Quality control was implemented by excluding 228/2343 (9.7%) healthy controls due to poor image quality or processing failures. For longitudinal data, only the highest quality image per child was used to ensure independence. Image processing used a symmetric template to ensure unbiased left-right hemisphere comparisons."
- normative_modeling.nm_vldtn_same_domain_nonindep_detail: "The study did not perform validation of the normative model using held-out healthy control data from the training set. No model performance metrics (e.g., explained variance, MSLL, correlation, skewness, kurtosis) were reported for the normative model fit to healthy controls. Cross-validation or train-test splitting within the healthy cohort was not described."
- normative_modeling.nm_vldtn_same_domain_indep_detail: "The study did not use an independent external cohort of healthy children to validate the normative model. All 2115 healthy children were used for training, with no external validation dataset reserved or acquired."
- normative_modeling.nm_vldtn_diff_domain_detail: "The normative model was applied to 57 pediatric epilepsy patients (29 left hemispherotomy, 28 right hemispherotomy) with both pre- and postoperative MRI scans. Different-domain validation was demonstrated through multiple lines of evidence: (1) Individual-level deviation patterns were biologically interpretable, showing widespread negative Z-scores indicating GM underdevelopment in patients (79% of left and 71% of right hemispherotomy patients had >80% voxels with negative preoperative Z-scores). (2) Longitudinal changes detected by the model (increases in contralateral cerebrum and ipsilateral cerebellum; decreases in contralateral cerebellum and cingulate gyrus) were consistent with known neuroplastic mechanisms following hemispherotomy. (3) Clinical validity was demonstrated by significant associations between GMV deviation changes and behavioral improvements, particularly in the contralateral cerebellum for right hemispherotomy patients (r=-0.64, p=0.007). (4) Predictive validity was established: preoperative GMV deviation patterns significantly predicted postoperative behavioral outcomes using machine learning (RR: left r=0.52 p=0.006, right r=0.48 p=0.021; SVR: left r=0.45 p=0.012, right r=0.46 p=0.021; LASSO: left r=0.48 p=0.008, right r=0.47 p=0.024). These findings collectively demonstrate successful generalization of the normative model to a clinically distinct patient population."
